             "/tx_not_before_s": "s"\n            },\n            "parent_sha256": "77de2f78de57912b69bdfaac075d0b918821b0d3879adddccb2322e7df0319b2",\n            "payload": {\n              "boot_delay_s": 0.001,\n              "boot_j": 5e-06,\n              "cap_initial_voltage_v": 0,\n              "cap_voltage_max_v": 3.3,\n              "capacitance_f": 2.2e-05,\n              "esr_ohm": 0.3,\n              "event_latch": "passive-mechanical",\n              "harvest_efficiency": 0.9,\n              "id": 260,\n              "idle_power_w": 0.0002,\n              "off_power_w": 2e-05,\n              "off_v": 1.8,\n              "output_v": 1.8,\n              "ready_v": 2.8,\n              "tx_current_a": 0.008,\n              "tx_duration_s": 0.002,\n              "tx_not_before_s": 0\n            },\n            "source_id": "component_record",\n            "source_sha256": "eeb8277f7faa548e82ac297fa65a5e17960824ad13ea20586108ba441280b212"\n          },\n          "ok": true,\n          "time": 1792440770.264\n        },\n        {\n          "type": "assistant_public",\n          "content": [\n            {\n              "type": "text",\n              "text": "I have the facts. Let me lay out the analysis before submitting.\\n\\n**Observed facts (versioned):**\\n- `inspect`: world `b-pulse-fast`, model `b-transient-behavior-v1`, current design C=22 \xc2\xb5F, tx_not_before=0; source 7.5 mW over [0, 0.02) s, harvest 0.9, dt=1e-4; service = 8 application bytes, 1 unique frame, deadline 0.1 s.\\n- `component_record` (sha eeb8277f\xe2\x80\xa6): eon=ready_v 2.8 V, eoff 1.8 V, emax 3.3 V, boot_j 5 \xc2\xb5J, boot_delay 1 ms, tx 1.8 V \xc3\x97 8 mA \xc3\x97 2 ms, esr 0.3 \xce\xa9, passive-mechanical latch.\\n- `saved_activity` (sha a4c4b294\xe2\x80\xa6): boot 0.0129 s, tx-start 0.0139 s, **tx-complete 0.0158 s**, packet 8 app bytes (`010400010101b0260657`, loaded_v 2.58 V), then brownout at **0.3395 s** with `event_was_captured=false`.\\n\\n**Reflection on the apparent failure:** the only \\"failure-looking\\" record is the 0.3395 s brownout. In the model law, `done` is set at tx-complete, and the source segment ends at 0.02 s, so that brownout is *post-terminal decay*, not a delivery failure. Delivery was already achieved at 0.0158 s, i.e. 84 ms inside the deadline. The likely methodological mistake is misclassifying post-terminal activity as a failure and spending one of the two candidate executions on a \\"fix\\" that moves the design the wrong way: the binding constraint here is not boot latency (huge deadline slack) but TX energy headroom. My ledger check shows the model\'s loaded-voltage guard binds at E_floor = C\xc2\xb7(1.8+0.0024)\xc2\xb2/2, so C at the legal floor (~4 \xc2\xb5F) yields a mid-TX brownout while C=22 \xc2\xb5F keeps ~2\xc3\x97 margin; a positive tx_not_before would only burn idle energy. So the *device* needs no change \xe2\x80\x94 but the *procedure* has a demonstrated gap. I submit one H update, not a device assignment."\n            },\n            {\n              "type": "toolCall",\n              "id": "call_00_lV6SBSKcP8YO7Dw095fj4039",\n              "name": "bf_phase3",\n              "arguments": {\n                "action": "submit",\n                "proposal": {\n                  "schema_version": "bf-phase2-method-proposal-v1",\n                  "decision": "propose",\n                  "changed_artifact": "H",\n                  "hypothesis": "The retained record contains one ACHIEVED delivery (tx-complete 0.0158 s, 8 application bytes, deadline 0.1 s) plus one post-terminal brownout at 0.3395 s whose packet was already sent and whose source segment ended at 0.02 s. The likely procedural error is not a bad design but a bad reading: the base H diagnoses the last activity record as a failure and can spend one of two candidate executions suppressing benign post-terminal decay, in a direction (lower capacitance, or a positive tx_not_before) that reduces TX energy headroom and can cause a real mid-transmission brownout. Hypothesis: a terminality-first diagnosis step plus a pre-registered analytic energy ledger with explicit feasibility inequalities will prevent that regression, keep each execution numerically falsifiable, and leave the base design unchanged when no hard inequality is violated. This is a procedure claim under the frozen model and recorded facts only; physical calibration stays UNKNOWN.",\n                  "falsifier": "The update is falsified if any of the following holds on the same frozen inputs and model id: (a) the ledger\'s predicted tx-complete time deviates from the observed packet time by more than 2*dt (2e-4 s); (b) a design that satisfies inequalities (i) deadline, (ii) TX headroom above the loaded-voltage floor, and (iii) no-spill, is observed to fail to deliver one 8-byte unique frame by the deadline; (c) a design that violates inequality (ii) is observed to deliver the frame without a tx-transient reset; (d) in a fresh session applying only this procedure, a post-done brownout is again classified as a delivery failure or consumes a candidate execution. Any of (a)-(d) means revert to the base method and log the mismatch and the actual executions spent.",\n                  "method_text": "H update v2: terminality-first diagnosis + pre-registered energy ledger gate.\\n\\n0. Scope guard. All conclusions are predictions of the frozen public model (model id b-transient-behavior-v1) and the recorded public facts; physical calibration remains UNKNOWN. Never edit the task, deadline, model law, evaluator or service fields to obtain a pass. This H governs only how I order experiments and read observations. It is not a device assignment and not an acceptance claim; acceptance is external.\\n\\n1. Terminality-first reading of retained observations, before any diagnosis.\\n1.1 Mark a delivery ACHIEVED if a packet exists with application_bytes equal to the service requirement, a single unique event_id, and packet time <= service deadline.\\n1.2 Mark every activity event whose time is strictly after the last tx-complete as POST-TERMINAL. Post-terminal brownouts, spills and idles are recorded as expected decay once the source segment ends; they are not failures and are never a reason to change a knob.\\n1.3 Only an absent achieved packet, or an achieved packet that violates a service field, counts as a retained failure. Classify it as: (a) deadline miss, (b) no boot (energy/threshold domain), (c) tx-transient brownout, (d) frame-shape violation (bytes, uniqueness, count).\\n1.4 Check the trace\'s energy bookkeeping for conservation on the recorded dt grid, tolerance one tick, and record any residual as unknown rather than smoothing it away.\\n\\n2. Pre-registered energy ledger, computed before an execution is spent; it is a prediction, not a result.\\nFrom the component record (C, ready_v, off_v, cap_voltage_max_v, boot_j, boot_delay_s, esr_ohm, output_v, tx_current_a, tx_duration_s, off_power_w, idle_power_w, harvest_efficiency) and the excitation record (segment power, start, end, dt):\\n- thresholds: E_on = C*ready_v^2/2, E_off = C*off_v^2/2, E_max = C*cap_voltage_max_v^2/2;\\n- charge step while unpowered = (segment power * harvest_efficiency - off_power_w) * dt;\\n- transmission floor from the model\'s own loaded-voltage guard, not from E_off: E_floor = C*(output_v + esr_ohm*tx_current_a)^2/2, which binds slightly above E_off during TX;\\n- TX demand E_tx = output_v*tx_current_a*tx_duration_s, minus harvest credit P*harvest_efficiency*tx_duration_s and idle drain over the same window;\\n- predictions: n_boot = ceil((E_on + boot_j)/charge_step), t_ready = n_boot*dt + boot_delay_s, t_done = t_ready + ceil(tx_duration_s/dt)*dt.\\nInequalities, all three logged with numeric margins: (i) t_done <= deadline_s; (ii) stored energy at ready minus E_floor plus net TX credit minus E_tx >= 0, so TX completes without a tx-transient reset; (iii) predicted peak stored energy in the frame window <= E_max, so no spill corrupts the charge budget.\\n\\n3. Guarded change control.\\n3.1 If the retained observation is ACHIEVED and the base design satisfies (i)-(iii) with recorded positive margins, RETAIN the base design and spend no execution. Explicitly record post-terminal events as benign.\\n3.2 If an inequality is violated, change exactly one legal knob, in the direction the sign of that inequality demands, re-evaluate all three inequalities, and only then spend one candidate execution.\\n3.3 Never change a knob in response to a post-terminal event; never change two knobs in one execution; respect the recorded execution and distinct-design budget and record the actual cost.\\n3.4 Keep a capture/timing knob at its earliest legal setting unless a recorded failure class names a capture gate, because a positive delay both spends idle energy and moves t_done toward the deadline.\\n\\n4. Falsification and logging.\\n4.1 For each execution, log the predicted t_boot, t_ready, t_done, the three margins and the input versions, then compare against the observation. A deviation beyond two dt ticks, or an inequality that was signed the wrong way, is a gate failure.\\n4.2 On gate failure, revert to the base method, record the failure class and the executions actually spent.\\n4.3 Keep failures, unknowns and costs visible; never re-label a failed or unknown observation as success, and never claim hardware validity, statistical advantage, migration or self-improvement from this procedure.",\n                  "changes": [\n                    {\n                      "artifact": "H",\n                      "component": "observation_policy"\n                    },\n                    {\n                      "artifact": "H",\n                      "component": "experiment_order"\n                    },\n                    {\n                      "artifact": "H",\n                      "component": "global_prompt"\n                    }\n                  ],\n                  "evide