d8c9ff82d9a",\n                "model_law:b-transient-behavior-v1",\n                "saved_activity.packets.0.time_s",\n                "saved_activity.packets.0.application_bytes",\n                "saved_activity.activity.2",\n                "saved_activity.activity.3",\n                "excitation_record.source_segments.0.end_s",\n                "inspect.legal_design_ranges",\n                "inspect.service.deadline_s"\n              ]\n            }\n          },\n          "result": {\n            "method_decision": "propose",\n            "method_sha256": "8434c18a49cb8ba91e98968435c33f76afaf446179f31ef89312a821e8716419",\n            "recorded": "submit"\n          },\n          "ok": true,\n          "time": 1792440815.012\n        },\n        {\n          "type": "assistant_public",\n          "content": [\n            {\n              "type": "text",\n              "text": "Submitted (method_sha256 `8434c18a\xe2\x80\xa66419`, `changed_artifact=H`, recorded as submit).\\n\\n**Public reasoning (concise, version-referenced)**\\n\\n1. *What the facts say.* From `inspect` (interface packet, model `b-transient-behavior-v1`, world `b-pulse-fast`, legal range C\xe2\x88\x88[4 \xc2\xb5F, 22 \xc2\xb5F], tx_not_before\xe2\x88\x88[0, 0.045] s) and the two acquires I actually made \xe2\x80\x94 `component_record` (sha `eeb8277f\xe2\x80\xa6`) and `saved_activity` (sha `a4c4b294\xe2\x80\xa6`) \xe2\x80\x94 the retained record shows **delivery already achieved**: tx-complete at **0.0158 s** with an 8-byte packet, against a **0.1 s** deadline, then a brownout at **0.3395 s** with `event_was_captured=false`.\\n\\n2. *Why the previous approach \\"failed\\".* It did not fail physically. The failure-looking artifact is post-terminal: `done` is set at tx-complete, the source segment ends at 0.02 s (per `excitation_record`), so the 0.3395 s brownout is expected decay. Treating it as a defect is the mistake \xe2\x80\x94 and it is an expensive one, because the two candidate executions would be spent moving a knob in the direction that *shrinks* margin. Per the model law the binding floor during transmission is the loaded-voltage guard E_floor = C\xc2\xb7(output_v + esr\xc2\xb7I)\xc2\xb2/2 \xe2\x89\x88 1.624\xc2\xb7C, while the TX demand is a fixed 1.8 V\xc2\xb78 mA\xc2\xb72 ms = 28.8 \xc2\xb5J; low C therefore produces a genuine mid-TX brownout, and a positive tx_not_before only burns idle energy and pushes t_done toward the deadline. So the honest reading is: no device change is warranted, and I did **not** encode one (the method contains no parameter assignment).\\n\\n3. *The procedural update (H only).* Terminality-first reading (classify post-done events as benign before drawing any conclusion), then a pre-registered energy ledger with three logged inequalities (deadline, TX headroom above the loaded-voltage floor, no-spill), one-knob change control, and prediction-vs-observation logging at two-tick tolerance.\\n\\n4. *Falsifier kept sharp:* ledger t_done off by >2\xc2\xb7dt (2e-4 s); an inequality-satisfying design failing to deliver; an inequality-violating design delivering without a tx-transient reset; or a fresh session reclassifying a post-done brownout as a failure and spending an execution on it.\\n\\n5. *Costs and honesty.* This session: 2 `inspect`/`sources` reads, 2 source acquires, 0 native dispatches, 0 new scientific executions, 0 candidate executions spent, no device submitted, no acceptance claimed. Scope statement `Frozen ideal digital BF apparatus; physical calibration UNKNOWN` is carried verbatim into the method \xe2\x80\x94 no hardware validity, statistical advantage, migration, or RSI is claimed; independent service acceptance remains external."\n            }\n          ],\n          "time": 1792440819.623\n        },\n        {\n          "type": "bf_episode_complete",\n          "episode": "p3-formal-1-1-ordinary_reflection-h",\n          "time": 1792440819.624\n        }\n      ],\n      "public_trace_canonical